1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,050
Look around your class: unless there are identical twins, everyone looks different.

2
00:00:05,050 --> 00:00:10,522
Variation is the differences between members of the same species, like height, eye colour,

3
00:00:10,522 --> 00:00:15,212
earlobes or blood group. Some variation, like blood group, can't even be seen.

4
00:00:16,272 --> 00:00:22,164
Individuals of a species share almost the same DNA, but small differences in DNA cause variation.

5
00:00:22,164 --> 00:00:27,996
Each gamete carries a different half set of DNA, so every fertilised egg gets a new combination.

6
00:00:27,996 --> 00:00:29,620
That's inherited variation.

7
00:00:30,680 --> 00:00:33,024
Not all variation is genetic. Climate,

8
00:00:33,024 --> 00:00:38,676
nutrition and soil also matter: hydrangeas are blue in acidic soil and pink in alkaline soil,

9
00:00:38,676 --> 00:00:43,667
exercise builds muscles, and sunshine darkens skin. That's environmental variation.

10
00:00:44,727 --> 00:00:50,679
A class of thirty students measured their heights and grouped them into ranges of five centimetres.

11
00:00:51,739 --> 00:00:56,128
Most students are in the middle groups, between 150 and 159 centimetres.

12
00:00:56,128 --> 00:00:58,473
Only a few are very short or very tall.

13
00:00:59,533 --> 00:01:06,027
Pause and decide: is height an example of continuous or discontinuous variation, and is it caused by genes,

14
00:01:06,027 --> 00:01:07,530
the environment, or both?
